<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burgos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burgos is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, capital of the province of Burgos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León. Founded in 884 as a frontier fortress by Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-rodriguez-porcelos">Diego Rodríguez Porcelos</a>, it became the capital of the County of Castile and long the chief city of the Kingdom of Castile, prospering on the wool trade and the pilgrim road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>. Its Gothic cathedral is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, and in 1936 it was the seat of Franco's rebel government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burgos.jpg" alt="Burgos" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jardoz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burgos_city_view_facing_south_east.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Burgos lies in the north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, near the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arlanzon-river">Arlanzón</a> and the Vena rivers and at the edge of the central plateau, at an elevation of 865 metres. Early humans occupied sites around Burgos as early as 800,000 years ago, and the nearby site of Atapuerca, 20 kilometres to the east, has yielded fossils and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> of the earliest known hominids in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> took possession the site had been a Celtic city. The name Burgos probably reflects the Visigothic word for walled villages, from the Gothic baurgs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> drove back the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> in the 5th century and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> briefly held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in the 8th, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias">Alfonso III of Asturias</a> conquered the area about the middle of the 9th century and built castles, from which Castile took its name, the land of castles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-rodriguez-porcelos">Diego Rodríguez Porcelos</a> gathered the inhabitants of the surrounding country into one fortified village in 884, and the city began to be called Caput Castellae, Head of Castile. In the 11th century it became an archdiocesan see and capital of the Kingdom of Castile. It was a major stop for pilgrims on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camino-frances">French Way</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> and a centre of trade, attracting a large foreign merchant population that joined the city <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>.</p><p>In the 13th and 14th centuries Burgos was a favourite seat of the kings of León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and a favoured burial site. Royal charters of 1255 and 1266 relieved from taxes those citizens who owned horses and could arm themselves. In 1285 Sancho IV added a body called the jurado to the town council, and on 9 June 1345 Alfonso XI established direct royal rule through the Regimiento of sixteen appointed men. In 1574 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xiii">Pope Gregory XIII</a> made the bishopric a metropolitan archbishopric at the request of Philip II. During the Peninsular War Wellington withdrew from the siege of Burgos, and the castle was destroyed by the French in 1813.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Burgos is the capital of its province in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León and its second most populated city. Construction of its Gothic cathedral began in 1221 and spanned mainly the 13th to 15th centuries; it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> and holds the remains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> and his wife Doña Jimena. The city also has the monastery of Las Huelgas, founded in 1180 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a>, and the Carthusian monastery of Miraflores, with the tombs of Juan II and Queen Isabella of Portugal. The Museum of Human Evolution, opened in 2010, draws on the Atapuerca site. The University of Burgos was established in 1994.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burgos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fine wool, shipped from the Cantabrian ports by the merchants of <b>Burgos</b> to Flanders, became Castile's leading export, and the fairs of Medina del Campo made it a centre of European finance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Fortified towns at Zamora, from 893, Simancas, Toro and <b>Burgos</b> anchored a frontier along the Douro, and the eastern march became known as Castile, the land of castles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Sheep-raising flourished on the empty plains, with flocks moving between summer and winter pastures, and <b>Burgos</b>, on the pilgrim road to Santiago de Compostela, grew into a trading town with many French settlers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Upper Navarre, south of the Pyrenees, was incorporated into the Crown of Castile by the cortes of <b>Burgos</b> in 1515, keeping its own laws, cortes and coinage under a viceroy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Promulgated at <b>Burgos</b> after friars and academics pressed Ferdinand II and Joanna of Castile, the Laws of Burgos were the first codified laws on the conduct of Spaniards toward the Indigenous peoples of the Americas.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-27">27 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Autrigones occupied the land from the upper Ebro to the coast around Castro Urdiales, in modern <b>Burgos</b>, Álava and Bizkaia, from the later Iron Age.</blockquote><a href="/state/autrigones">Autrigones</a></li></ul></section>
